x, doc)) fz_throw(...)`. So if objstm_num >= xref_len, it throws \"object out of range\". That throw propagates up through pdf_load_obj_stm's fz_try, caught by... pdf_load_obj_stm has `objstm = pdf_load_object(...)` in a fz_try with fz_catch that does `pdf_drop_obj(ctx, objstm); fz_rethrow(ctx)`. So it rethrows. Then in pdf_cache_object's 'o' branch fz_catch rethrows, but fz_always runs `orig_x->type = 'o'`. If solidification happened before the throw... \n\nHmm, actually pdf_get_xref_entry_no_null is called AFTER the range check in pdf_cache_object. The range check `num >= pdf_xref_len` throws first. pdf_xref_len doesn't solidify. So no realloc before throw. So orig_x still valid. No UAF.\n\nOK so the range check prevents that. Let me think differently.\n\nLet me reconsider: the reallocation must happen DURING pdf_load_obj_stm successfully (or in a way that frees orig_x's table). \n\nThe contained objects loop: `entry = pdf_get_xref_entry_no_null(ctx, doc, numbuf[i])`. numbuf[i] < xref_len (checked). For this to solidify, the entry must not be found in any section with non-zero type, AND not be in the base section's subsec range. \n\nHmm, when would numbuf[i] < xref_len but not in any subsec? If the xref has multiple subsections with gaps. E.g., xref subsection [0..100] and [200..300], and numbuf[i]=150. Then 150 < xref_len(301) but not in any subsec range. Then pdf_get_xref_entry_aux falls through all sections (entry not found), then the final part: `xref = &doc->xref_sections[doc->xref_base]`, loop subsecs — 150 not in [0..100] or [200..300], so not returned. Then... `if (doc->xref_sections[doc->num_xref_sections-1].num_objects == 0) return NULL;` — no, num_objects != 0. Then `if (!solidify_if_needed) return NULL;` — solidify_if_needed=1 (pdf_get_xref_entry). So `ensure_solid_xref(ctx, doc, i+1, 0)` — REALLOCATION! This creates a new solid table covering [0..i+1], frees old sub->table. orig_x dangles!\n\nSo the trigger: an object stream whose contained object list includes a number that falls in a GAP of the xref subsections (valid range but not covered by any subsection). Then pdf_get_xref_entry_no_null(numbuf[i]) solidifies, reallocating the table, freeing orig_x's table. Then back in pdf_cache_object, `orig_x->type = 'o'` (fz_always) writes to freed memory -> UAF.\n\nBut wait — does the contained object loop run BEFORE or AFTER the reallocation matters? The reallocation happens during the loop. After the loop, pdf_load_obj_stm returns ret_entry. Then in pdf_cache_object, fz_always runs `orig_x->type = 'o'`. orig_x is dangling -> UAF write. \n\nBut actually, hold on. After solidification, the entry for `num` (the target, the 'o' entry) is now in the NEW solid table. But orig_x still points to the OLD freed table. So `orig_x->type = 'o'` is a UAF write. And `x` (returned from pdf_load_obj_stm = ret_entry) points into the NEW table (it was obtained via pdf_get_xref_entry_no_null after solidification, or before?). Let me check: ret_entry is set inside the loop: `if (numbuf[i] == target) ret_entry = entry;` where entry = pdf_get_xref_entry_no_null(numbuf[i]) — obtained AFTER solidification (since solidification happens during that call). So ret_entry points into the new table. Good, so `x` (the return) is valid, but `orig_x` is dangling. The fz_always `orig_x->type = 'o'` is the UAF.\n\nHmm wait, but actually there's subtlety: the solidification happens when processing numbuf[i] that's in a gap. But the target `num` (the 'o' entry we're caching) — is `num` in a gap too? No, `num` is the 'o' entry, which exists (we got orig_x from pdf_get_xref_entry(num) successfully). So `num` is in a valid subsec. After solidification, `num`'s entry is copied to the new solid table. orig_x pointed to old table (freed). \n\nSo the trigger PDF needs:\n1. An xref with subsections that have a GAP (e.g., a classic xref with multiple subsections, or an xref stream).\n2. An object stream (ObjStm) referenced via an 'o' xref entry.\n3. The ObjStm's object number list includes a number that falls in the gap.\n\nActually, creating a classic xref with gaps is straightforward. A classic xref table can have multiple subsections:\n\n```\nxref\n0 2\n0000000000 65535 f \n0000000009 00000 n \n5 1\n0000000200 00000 n \n```\n\nThis creates subsection [0..2) (objects 0,1) and [5..6) (object 5). Gap at 2,3,4. xref_len = 6.\n\nNow, object 5 could be an object stream. Its 'o' entries... wait, the 'o' type is set when an object is stored in an object stream. In a classic xref, you can't directly have 'o' type entries — 'o' type comes from xref streams (cross-reference streams). \n\nHmm. Actually, the 'o' type entries are created when parsing an xref stream (cross-reference stream) where the entry type is 2 (compressed). Let me verify.\n\nLet me look at how 'o' entries are created.",
